A||N||13||Seanh||Few question about my front end rebuild.||||5||
Z||000000||Seanh||06-28-2001||12:40 AM||||Ok, I have the drivers side completly disassembled. I have just gotten done painting the upper control arm, and have installed the new upper ball joint and poly bushings. <P>First question: I bought the ball joints FieroStore sells. The old one I took off (stock I believe) has a round bolt hole, and the one I got from FieroStore has oval bolt holes, so theres alot of room for adjustment. How to I align it reasonably close first off? Once this projected is completed I will take it and have a complete front end alignment done.<P>Second Q: I also bought the FieroStores front end control arm poly bushing kit. I has 12 peices. 4 are the bushings for the upper contol arms, 4 are for the lower control arms. My ? is what are the 4 ring looking bushings for? I don't recall ever seeing them, but all my bushings were stock, so this may be something that wasn't there before. Anyone know?<P>Third Q: I just took the rotor off the front hub. I bought all new wheel bearings and seals for replacment also. The new bearings come with journals (I guess) that go inside the rotor. I see my old ones in there, but can't get them out, or to even budge. First off, do I even need to replace them? I guess I might as well since they come with the new bearings don't ya think? How do I get the old out and the new in?<P>Guess that's it for now. I'm sure I'll have some more further into this project.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||06-28-2001||01:03 AM||||First ?<BR>Your assumption is correct on the adjustment with the new upper ball joints. If you can tell the placement may have been on the factory ones set it there. If not just give it a guess since you need an alignment anyway.<P>Second ?<BR>Sorry, no help!<P>Third ?<BR>Use a small punch from the opposite direction. Clean up the inside of them real good and you can see the lip on the races that you can use the punch on. When you put in the new ones, make sure you get them started squarely and use a hammer to seat them as far as you can (walk the hammer head across the bearing race in a cross and circular motion) and use the punch to get them in the rest of the way.<BR>Remember to pack them well with high temp wheel bearing grease before you set the rotors back on the spindle.<P>Good luck!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||06-28-2001||01:52 AM||||First, When you put the upper ball joints on the top, Center them in the holes. Then after everything is together, lower the car and roll it forward and back. Take a level and place it between the top and bottom edge of the wheel, see if the wheel is a level 90* to the ground. If not raise adjust as needed and repeat the measurement.  This isn't perfect but will get it close enough to drive safely to the alignment shop.<P>Second, The kit from the Fiero Store is a universal kit, It covers  a few different cars. Just replace what you removed.<P>Third, What Resto_guy said.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">     <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||06-28-2001||08:12 AM||||for question number 2, I know the '88s use all 12. 4 for the top, 8 on the bottom. the 4 cylendrical ones get pressed into the sleve and stuck into the A-frame, the donut shaped ones go behind them, between the frame and the sleeve to the the control arms from sliding to the front and back. if it's a pre '88... no idea.<P>-Rick||||reg||1
Z||000004||Steve Normington||06-28-2001||12:35 PM||||2) I replaced the bushings on my 85 SE and I used the donuts on the opposite side of the bushing (the inside of the control arm). Everything bolted up just fine, but I haven't run the car yet.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||06-28-2001||01:24 PM||||#1 - Take a guess. The center line of the upper ball joint should be slightly behind the center line of the lower ball joint. If you have very far to drive before you get an alignment, I would drive it around the block then look to see if the front tires at least look like they are vertical (0 camber). If not I'd adjust it as close as possible by eye, or even use a level. Also, check the toe-in. Measure across the front and back of the tread at some equal point off the ground. The front measurement should be slightly (1/16" or so) less than the back. If not, adjust the tie rod ends.<P>#2 - I used them as Steve said on my 88. The instructions called them thrust bushings I think. They are supposed to reduce forward-backward movement of the lower control arm. They should fit snuggly over the small end of the bushing after it's installed in the control arm.<P>#3 - What Indy said...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||06-28-2001||05:36 PM||||Ok, I understand this now. But, on those 4 rings that came in the suspension bushing pack. There is a small hole on one side, and larger on the other, ok. Does the small hole(one that fits on the back of control arm bushing) go twords the front of the car, or back? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||06-28-2001||06:06 PM||||The way I did mine the small hole goes away from the control arm. On an 88 at least that means it's facing the back of the car on one bushing and the front on the other.<P>After it's on it should make a surface that is (about) flush with the end of the small part of the bushing sticking thru the control arm. It should make the small end of the bushing look about like the large end. <P>Hard to explain... maybe someone can post pictures???||||reg||1
Z||000008||Leadfoot||06-28-2001||06:10 PM||||The rings should fit over the front side of the lower control arm bushings.  The small hole should point towards the front of the car and the large hole towards the control arm.  Essentially it fits over the smaller diameter end of the bushing.  Look at the cartoon below.<P>----^^^^^^^^^^^^******<BR>- ********************<BR>- ********************<P>- ********************<BR>- ********************<BR>----^^^^^^^^^^^^******<P>- is the ring<BR>* is the bushing<BR>^ control arm<BR>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>Ok, I understand this now. But, on those 4 rings that came in the suspension bushing pack. There is a small hole on one side, and larger on the other, ok. Does the small hole(one that fits on the back of control arm bushing) go twords the front of the car, or back? Thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>[This message has been edited by Leadfoot (edited 06-28-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Leadfoot (edited 06-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||06-28-2001||06:24 PM||||Alright, thats done. Lower are is ready to be installed now. <P>One more q: What is the configuration of spacers/washers on the shaft for the upper control arm? I have the shaft, 4 larger washers, and 2 smaller thicker washers and the nut. How should they be configured around the bushing and mounts? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000010||Leadfoot||06-28-2001||06:44 PM||||Well my upper control arm had 2 thick washers that sat in between the control arm bushings and the bolt sleeve connected that is connected to the body.  I think the hanyes manual also showed it that way.  As for the larger washers you put two at each end.  So you put two at the head of the bolt and two at the nut.  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||DRH||06-28-2001||06:48 PM||||Nice ASCII picture there leadfoot! That's exactly what I was trying to explain. ||||reg||14
Z||000012||Seanh||06-28-2001||11:49 PM||||Ok, upper and lower contol arms are bolted on and torqued down to specs. Now comes the fun part, re-installing the coil spring. Anyone have any tips that might make this a little easier? I am now thinking about first putting the spring in the lower control arm, then forcing it over the upper spring seat, will that work? ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Leadfoot||06-29-2001||10:36 AM||||I tried that and it doesn't work.  It is also not very safe since the upper bumps stop does a much better job of holding the spring in place.||||reg||1
